    "body": "<p>This Signature model designs a river soundscape as a flow-dependent masking system. Virtual river noise is specified by flow level, virtual birds and frogs are specified by call levels and bands, and the model computes the signal-to-noise ratio for each virtual voice at low, medium, and high flow, showing which voices survive spate and which are masked. A call-design parameter lets a student raise a virtual voice&#x27;s frequency above the water-noise band and watch its masking margin improve. The river is invented, the voices are virtual, and any rendered river sound is labelled SIMULATED. Its purpose is teaching masking by geophony and giving composers a river template that changes with flow.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: the model encodes the documented river pattern: water-noise spectra that rise with flow, masking of animal calls at high flow, and call adaptation to the water-noise band in torrent species. Those patterns are published river bioacoustics findings, and the model&#x27;s signal-to-noise arithmetic is the standard masking calculation.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if virtual river noise measures 60 dB and a virtual call measures 66 dB in the same band, the signal-to-noise ratio is 66 - 60 = 6 dB, meaning the call stands above the noise. The demo prints signal-to-noise 6 dB for the 66 dB call, -2 dB for a 58 dB call, and the combined level of river and 66 dB call, 66.97 dB.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Signature design study for this database, October 2026; river masking pattern as in the public edition</p>",
